# Binary formats, declared rather than guessed.
#
# This checkout has core.autocrlf=true, so Git rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout for every file
# it believes is text. It decides that by sniffing the first few kilobytes for a NUL byte,
# which is a guess - and it guesses wrong on a PDF whose streams happen not to be compressed.
# Three of the weekly-report PDFs here were staged as text (99, 124 and 105 "lines"), meaning
# a later checkout would have inserted carriage returns into the file and produced a PDF that
# no longer opens. The catalogue PDFs, which are compressed, were correctly seen as binary -
# so the failure is silent and only hits some files, which is the worst kind.
#
# Naming them stops the sniffing: no line-ending conversion, and no attempt to diff them as
# text either.
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.docx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.dump  binary
*.zip   binary
*.tgz   binary

# Batch files must be CRLF. cmd.exe tolerates LF for plain commands but cannot resolve a
# `goto` label in an LF-only file - it reports "The system cannot find the batch label
# specified" and the window closes. run.bat had drifted to LF (its siblings had not), which
# stayed invisible only because it contained no labels until it needed one.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and git hooks must be LF - the mirror image of the rule above. With CRLF the
# first line reads "#!/bin/sh\r", and the hook fails with "bad interpreter" (or, worse, runs
# under a different shell and misbehaves). autocrlf=true would do exactly that on checkout.
*.sh        text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf
